<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Tychonoff space</h1>

<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<br class="ltx_break">
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p class="ltx_p">A <a class="nnexus_concepts" href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="this.nextSibling.style.display='inline'">topological space</a><sup style="display: none;"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/TopologicalSpace.html"><img src="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/favicon_mathworld.png" alt="Mathworld"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/topologicalspace"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a></sup> <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline"><mi>X</mi></math> is said to be <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><a class="nnexus_concepts" href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="this.nextSibling.style.display='inline'">completely regular</a><sup style="display: none;"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/separationaxioms"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/tychonoffspace"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a></sup></em> if whenever <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subseteq X" display="inline"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is closed and <math id="p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X\setminus C" display="inline"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></math> then there is a <a class="nnexus_concepts" href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="this.nextSibling.style.display='inline'">continuous function</a><sup style="display: none;"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/ContinuousFunction.html"><img src="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/favicon_mathworld.png" alt="Mathworld"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/classesofordinalsandenumeratingfunctions"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/continuous"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a></sup> <math id="p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to[0,1]" display="inline"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></math> with <math id="p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=0" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁢</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math id="p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(C)\subseteq\{1\}" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁢</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p class="ltx_p">A completely regular space that is also <math id="p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> (<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">http://planetmath.org/T0Space</span>) (and therefore <a class="nnexus_concepts" href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="this.nextSibling.style.display='inline'">Hausdorff</a><sup style="display: none;"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/hausdorffspace"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/separationaxioms"><img src="http://planetmath.org/sites/default/files/fab-favicon.ico" alt="Planetmath"></img></a></sup> (<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">http://planetmath.org/T2Space</span>))
is called a <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/TychonoffSpace.html">Tychonoff space</a></em>, or a <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline"><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn mathvariant="normal">3</mn><mo mathvariant="normal">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn mathvariant="normal">1</mn><mn mathvariant="normal">2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub></math> space</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para">
<p class="ltx_p">Some authors interchange the meanings of ‘completely regular’ and ‘<math id="p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline"><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub></math>’ compared to the above.</p>
</div>
<div id="p5" class="ltx_para">
<p class="ltx_p">It can be proved that a topological space is Tychonoff
if and only if it has a <a class="nnexus_concept" href="http://planetmath.org/compactification">Hausdorff compactification</a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p6" class="ltx_para ltx_align_right">
